flex on Windows (MSVC), which has no fork(): its output filters run one after the other.

flex sends what it writes through a chain of filters: a tee into the scanner and its header, m4 over each, and the fix
of the #line directives. Elsewhere each filter is a process of fork() reading the one before through a pipe, m4 one
of execvp(). On Windows:

- src/filter.c: filter_apply_chain() only puts the standard output of flex on a temporary file; filter_run_deferred()
  runs the chain over it when flex ends without an error, each filter from a temporary file into the next, the tee
  running the rest of the chain once for the scanner and once for the header, m4 with _spawnvp() and its standard
  input and output on the files. The temporary files and the outputs are binary, so the output has the line ends of
  flex elsewhere (LF); what m4 writes, as text (CR LF), is read back as text. The tee and the fix of the #line
  directives write streams given to them (tee_streams(), fix_linedirs_streams()), which the filters of fork() use too.
- src/main.c: flex_main() runs the chain where it waited for the children; without M4 in the environment flex runs
  ..\libexec\flex\m4.exe from the directory of the program, then m4.exe beside it, then the m4 of PATH (the M4 of
  configure is a path of the build machine); the tables file is written binary.
- src/flexdef.h: filter_run_deferred().

